Analyze the following {language} code for SOLID principle violations.

For each violation found, report:
- principle: S (Single Responsibility), O (Open/Closed), L (Liskov Substitution), I (Interface Segregation), or D (Dependency Inversion)
- line: approximate line number
- severity: high, medium, or low
- violation: what specific rule is broken
- suggestion: concrete, actionable fix

S — Single Responsibility: class/function has more than one reason to change (handles multiple unrelated concerns)
O — Open/Closed: code requires modification instead of extension to add behavior (switch/if-else on type instead of polymorphism)
L — Liskov Substitution: subclass changes behavior in a way that breaks parent contract (throws where parent doesn't, ignores params, changes postconditions)
I — Interface Segregation: interface forces implementors to depend on methods they don't use (fat interface)
D — Dependency Inversion: class directly instantiates concrete dependencies instead of depending on abstractions

